# Dynamic Programming and Greedy Algorithms: Choosing Well, Reusing Work

> When a problem asks for the best, the count, or the longest, two
> strategies dominate: dynamic programming reuses answers to
> overlapping subproblems; greedy commits to provably safe choices.

## 1. Two Ways to Optimize

Brute force tries every possibility — correct, but exponential:

- **Dynamic programming (DP)** enumerates each subproblem once and
  reuses the answers it already paid for.
- **Greedy algorithms** make one pass of local decisions and never
  look back — valid only when local optimality provably implies global.

## 2. When Dynamic Programming Applies

[definition]
A problem fits DP when it has **overlapping subproblems** (the same
smaller cases recur, like fib(3) in naive Fibonacci) and **optimal
substructure** (optimal answers build on optimal sub-answers), as in
shortest paths: the fastest route to a city extends a fastest route.
[/definition]

## 3. Memoization vs Tabulation

| Aspect | Memoization (top-down) | Tabulation (bottom-up) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Style | recursion plus a cache | fill a table with loops |
| Computes | only the states actually needed | every state up to n |
| Risk | deep call stack | must order the table correctly |
| Best at | sparse state spaces | dense, regular ones |

## 4. Fibonacci: From Exponential to Linear

```text
FIB-TAB(n):
  table[0] = 0, table[1] = 1
  for i in 2 .. n:
    table[i] = table[i-1] + table[i-2]
  return table[n]
```

Each value is computed once from the two below it: O(n) time, and O(1)
extra space if only the last two values are kept.

## 5. The 0/1 Knapsack, Walked Through

Items with weight and value, knapsack capacity 5:

| Item | Weight | Value |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | 2 | 3 |
| B | 3 | 4 |
| C | 4 | 5 |
| D | 5 | 6 |

Each cell holds the best value from the first i items within capacity
c: skip the item (value above) or take it (value plus best at c − weight).

| Items used \ Capacity | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| none | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| A | 0 | 0 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 3 |
| A, B | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 4 | 7 |
| A, B, C | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 7 |
| A, B, C, D | 0 | 0 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 7 |

Bottom-right: 7 — items A and B (weight 5). The table costs O(n·W).

[note]
The **0/1** rule — take an item whole or leave it — is what forces DP;
the **fractional knapsack** is greedily solvable instead.
[/note]

## 6. Longest Common Subsequence

Given S1 = "ACE" and S2 = "ABCDE", the LCS is "ACE". Each cell holds
the LCS length of a prefix of S1 against a prefix of S2: equal letters
extend the diagonal, else the better of dropping one character from
either string.

| | A | B | C | D | E |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| A | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| C | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 |
| E | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 |

Bottom-right: 3 ("ACE"). LCS costs O(m·n) — the engine inside diff tools.

## 7. Coin Change: Where Greedy Breaks

Greedy always takes the largest coin that fits. With coins {1, 3, 4}
and amount 6:

@diagram tree id="greedy-vs-optimal-coins" layout="tree" aria="Greedy coin change makes 6 as 4 plus 1 plus 1, while the optimum is 3 plus 3"
  "Make 6 from coins 1, 3, 4" -> "Greedy: 4 + 1 + 1 (3 coins)"
  "Make 6 from coins 1, 3, 4" -> "Optimal: 3 + 3 (2 coins)"
@/diagram

DP fixes it: min-coins(6) = 1 + min over coins of min-coins(6 − coin) —
O(n·k) overall. Greedy happens to work for "nice" systems like
{1, 2, 5, 10}, but only DP works for all of them.

## 8. Greedy Algorithms: Commit and Move On

[definition]
A problem has the **greedy-choice property** when a locally optimal
choice extends to a globally optimal solution. Then a **greedy
algorithm** — take the best local option, never revisit — is correct.
[/definition]

| Problem | Greedy rule | Why it is safe |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Activity selection | pick the activity that finishes earliest | it blocks the fewest others |
| Fractional knapsack | take items by best value per unit weight | fractions fill the remainder exactly |
| Huffman coding | merge the two rarest symbols first | rare symbols earn long codes |
| Dijkstra | settle the closest unsettled vertex | non-negative edges only |

## 9. Choosing the Right Tool

| Signal in the problem | Reach for |
| --- | --- |
| "Count the ways…", "minimum cost to reach…", "longest X" | dynamic programming |
| Local best choice provably safe | greedy |
| Subproblems disjoint, no overlap | divide and conquer |
| n tiny (under ~20), no structure found | brute force |

[warning]
Do not DP everything: if a greedy rule is provably correct, DP wastes
time and memory on it; if subproblems never overlap, plain
divide-and-conquer is simpler. Match the tool to the structure.
[/warning]

[summary]
DP = overlapping subproblems + optimal substructure; solve each state
once: Fibonacci O(n), knapsack O(n·W), LCS O(m·n). Greedy = safe
local choices: activity selection, fractional knapsack, Huffman,
Dijkstra. Coins {1, 3, 4} making 6 prove greedy needs a proof.
[/summary]
